{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789377906,"nonce":"nT1gLRWecpvi7Hp5fOSbOToJt6g=","message":"Direction (2026-09-14): at AOT a cross-language site has no bridge at all. `wren_call`, the host entry, the kinds and the direct sends exist because the callee is found at run time; at link time it is a symbol.\n\nMechanism:\n- One mangling, derived from the export: `bench:tally.Tally.add(_)` with `#export = \"add(x: Num) -\u003e Num\"` names a C symbol with the C signature the export types dictate (`double f(double)`). Caribou owns the rule — the registry's interface written as a symbol — and both AOTs derive from it, so the two sides agree by construction, as the macro and the publisher already agree on types.\n- wren_lift's AOT emits the definition: a C-ABI thunk per exported member that boxes the arguments (a Num is its own bits), calls the compiled body, unboxes the result. For an imported Haxe class it emits the reference, undefined: `Bench.add(s)` is `call haxe_bench_Bench_add_1`.\n- Ash's AOT emits the mirror: a `caribou` native is an extern C function of the declared signature under that name, and an exported Haxe static or method gets a C-ABI symbol by the same mangling. The published set seeds reachability (comment #1).\n- A standard linker resolves the forward references between the two object files; the build step (run both AOTs, link with lld) is the driver's. A custom linker is the tool only for wasm, where `ash_wasm_link` takes wren_lift's module too.\n- Both runtimes have LLVM backends: when both emit bitcode, lld with LTO inlines thunk and callee into the caller, so `Bench.add(s)` in Wren compiles to `s + 1`. That is the one-native-code outcome, and it needs the two AOTs to hand LLVM the same module, nothing more.\n\nWhat stays at run time: object identity (the shadow word and the record's descriptor are facts of the shared heap, not dispatch; a face's creation gets direct, its bookkeeping stays); the trap on Wren→Haxe calls until Ash's exceptions cross a native frame without longjmp, which the thunk arms meanwhile; untyped members (`Fn`, `Dynamic`, a parameter without a type), whose thunk calls today's bridge — so a development-time report can say per site \"links statically at AOT\" or \"stays dynamic: declare its type\".\n\nOrder: the mangling and thunk signatures as a caribou spec, since both runtimes implement against it; wren_lift's AOT emitting definitions and references; Ash's AOT exporting by the mangling; the driver linking the two; then bitcode and LTO. The exception model runs alongside, since it decides the Wren→Haxe thunk's shape. Worked example on both sides: the interop bench's `Tally` and `Bench`.","files":null}]}